cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  skeletal-breadth: analysis code for "How widely a gene acts across the
  skeleton predicts how widely its mutations cause disease"
message: >-
  If you use this software or the derived data in this repository, please cite
  both the software and the manuscript it accompanies.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Alexander
    family-names: Schulz
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0009-2605-4350"
    affiliation: >-
      Institute of Human Genetics, Universitätsklinikum Erlangen,
      Friedrich-Alexander-Universität Erlangen-Nürnberg, Erlangen, Germany
  - given-names: Christian T.
    family-names: Thiel
    email: Christian.Thiel@uk-erlangen.de
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3817-7277"
    affiliation: >-
      Institute of Human Genetics, Universitätsklinikum Erlangen,
      Friedrich-Alexander-Universität Erlangen-Nürnberg, Erlangen, Germany
abstract: >-
  Companion repository for a study of skeletal genetic architecture. For every
  gene, breadth is the number of six anatomically named DXA bone-mineral-
  density sites at which it acts. Breadth predicts whether a gene causes
  Mendelian skeletal disease, across three independent curations; it predicts
  how many body regions that disease extends over; it does not cross the organ
  boundary, which resolves the published phenome-wide null result for the
  correspondence between common-variant and Mendelian pleiotropy; and it
  replicates in an independent cohort and carries fracture risk. The repository
  reproduces every number and figure of the paper from public summary
  statistics.
keywords:
  - bone mineral density
  - Mendelian disease
  - pleiotropy
  - skeletal dysplasia
  - GWAS
  - MAGMA
  - human phenotype ontology
  - gene-level association
